The mother has a strong, specific duty to her young child—especially since leaving her alone has previously caused fear—so completely disregarding that obligation carries real moral cost. At the same time, encountering someone in visible pain right in front of you creates an immediate claim for some response; walking past without any acknowledgment fails to respect that person’s dignity, though the situation is not life-threatening. Because the stranger is conscious and not in imminent danger, she need not stay for a lengthy rescue, but she should not pretend she saw nothing either. A brief assessment and calling for help honors both claims: she mitigates harm to the stranger without catastrophically failing her child. So the best response balances these duties rather than treating either as absolute.

For reasoning, proximity genuinely generates an obligation to react (that is why ignoring the fallen person feels wrong), but it does not erase closer responsibilities to dependents. Meanwhile, universal human worth underlies why strangers matter at all, yet in this specific conflict it is less decisive than the direct pull of seeing suffering combined with prior parental commitment.

