The family’s awareness creates real ethical weight: choosing to benefit from known harm involves some complicity, so completely denying responsibility is unconvincing. At the same time, budget constraints are genuine, and one household’s purchases rarely shift corporate practices, meaning excessive individual blame misses the larger picture—weak regulations, lack of affordable alternatives, and corporate accountability are the root issues. So there is a moral cost to knowingly supporting exploitation, but the evaluation should emphasize indirect moral responsibility without ignoring structural realities, and it should recognize that fixing this requires systemic change rather than pure consumer guilt.

